  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through Saturday)\nIssued at 1227 AM CDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nMonday through Wednesday will be warm with upper level ridging in\nplace from central Texas into the southern Mississippi River \nvalley area. Highs will be in the upper 80s to the mid 90s, and \npotentially event some upper 90s in the Big Country. Lows will \nalso creep into the upper 60s to lower 70s as Gulf moisture \nadvects northwestward on southerly low level flow into our area, \nraising dewpoints back up into the 60s across most of the area. \n\nBy Tuesday evening, an upper level low is expected to be pushing \neastward through the central Rockies area and toward the Four \nCorners. This will start breaking down the ridging over the south,\nand possible offer some marginal relief in temperatures on \nWednesday. The other affect of this approaching low will be \nincreasing southwesterly flow aloft over west and west central \nTexas. With embedded shortwave energy interacting with the \nincreasing low level moisture, we could see precipitation chances \nback in our area Tuesday night into Wednesday, mainly west of a \nBrownwood to Sonora line. \n\nFrom Wednesday through the end of the week, operational models \nbegin to diverge in their solutions, creating quite a bit of \nuncertainty in the forecast. The GFS is much more progressive with\nthe upper low, ejecting it out into the central plains during the\nsecond half of the week, while the ECMWF cuts the low off from \nupper level flow, causing it to hang back over the Desert \nSouthwest area, possibly even retrograding to the west. Regardless\nof the upper level pattern to our west, both models agree that a \ncold front will move into the area Thursday night into Friday, \nwhich should give our area another chance at precipitation \nThursday night into Friday. Cooler temperatures are expected from \nFriday through next weekend.",
